db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354442 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d05c0df-0883-5373-84c3-aa2cb3f54a04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354442 #edb/ref 1074 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-3)-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-15-10 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+22.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3d8a5b7-428e-5b9f-a099-8d78abc215c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354445 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}+22I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =2{A}^{2}+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cddc84f4-8d27-54c1-a11e-f66ad824ed8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354447 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +29) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+12{\\lambda }^{2}-49\\lambda +58 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}(-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{2}+12A-49I+58{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62f9e5ba-0775-5b99-88ac-c3c5c53cda55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354448 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $+$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x-20\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $-$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $21x$ | $+$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(21x+20),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =21A+20I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186392145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392141 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8edebb12-abdf-545c-97fb-264d8989834b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392142 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392142 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392142 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "834aa185-6012-5fad-9cec-bfae8e36cc07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392143 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref